---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../../components/Ornament.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { stats, quotes } from "../../lib/portal-data.js";

const s = stats();
const q = quotes();
const exact = q.filter((x) => x.validation.method === "exact").length;
const fuzzy = q.filter((x) => x.validation.method === "fuzzy").length;
---

<Layout
  title="Method"
  description="The constraints this portal works under, the gate every quotation passes, and the two interpretive slides it is built to avoid."
  current="/method"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">Method</span>
    <h1>How this is built, and what it refuses to do</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Arthurian material invites two specific kinds of dishonesty, and most popular treatments
      commit at least one of them. This portal is built to make both harder.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The one rule that governs everything</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Name the version. Never flatten it.</strong> There is no &ldquo;the legend.&rdquo;
      Geoffrey&rsquo;s Arthur is born to rule and proves it by conquering Europe; Malory&rsquo;s is
      a boy nobody has heard of who pulls a sword from a stone because his brother forgot his.
      Wolfram&rsquo;s Grail is a stone healed by pity; the Queste&rsquo;s is a judge that most of
      the Round Table fails. These are not variant tellings of one story. They are incompatible
      answers to shared questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every claim on this site is attached to a voice, and the tradition each voice belongs to
      carries a colour that follows it everywhere &mdash; on a quotation, in the margin of a chapter,
      on the crosswalk. Where two authors contradict each other, the contradiction is drawn rather
      than averaged. Collapsing them into a generic &ldquo;Arthurian myth&rdquo; is the one thing
      this portal will not do.
    </p>

    <h2>The second rule: count the cost</h2>
    <p>
      This is a literature of tragic expenditure, not of triumph. Every excellence in it depletes
      something: the Grail empties the fellowship, the oath breeds the violence it forbids, the love
      that makes the greatest knight cracks the kingdom, the foreknowledge that sees everything
      saves no one. So every story and every throughline here carries a panel asking what its
      subject <em>costs</em>, and the build fails if one is missing. An account of Camelot that does
      not count the price has misread the canon.
    </p>

    <h2>Verification is the product</h2>
    <p>
      Every quotation published here is located in the source text it claims to come from &mdash;
      not once, but on every build. The check normalises away the noise that defeats naive matching
      (Project Gutenberg&rsquo;s italic markers, editorial footnote numbers, thorn and yogh, and the
      ordinary drift of scanned text) and then requires either an exact match or a fuzzy match above
      a threshold set per source. A quotation that cannot be found does not ship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Of the {q.length} quotations currently published, {exact} are located exactly and {fuzzy} by
      fuzzy match against scanned originals. You can see the whole ledger, with the verdict on each
      one, at <a href={withBase("/quotes/")}>the quote ledger</a> &mdash; and where a work is
      published here, each quotation links to the exact paragraph it sits in, so you can check it
      yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>Two slides this portal is built to avoid</h2>
    <h3>The mysticism slide</h3>
    <p>
      The commonest failure in writing about this material is to dissolve specific, conflicting
      texts into one timeless wisdom &mdash; the Grail as an eternal archetype, Camelot as a
      parable for the self, the whole thing as a journey the reader is secretly on. It moves from
      <em>what a particular author wrote in a particular decade</em> to <em>what the myth eternally
      means for you</em>, and in doing so it erases the differences that are the entire subject.
      Whenever a sentence here begins to sound like &ldquo;the myth tells us that we must&rdquo;, it
      has gone wrong. The retellings on this site narrate. They do not moralise.
    </p>
    <h3>The pseudo-history slide</h3>
    <p>
      The second is the hunt for the real King Arthur: treating Geoffrey of Monmouth&rsquo;s fiction
      as recoverable Dark-Age fact, or enlisting the legend for an ethnic origin story. Geoffrey
      <em>was</em> propaganda, and skilful propaganda; this portal studies how the historical Arthur
      was manufactured, and declines to join the manufacture. The standing rebuke is Gildas, the one
      witness who might have known, who describes the victory at Badon and never mentions Arthur at
      all.
    </p>

    <h2>Each text keeps its own metaphysics</h2>
    <p>
      The Grail is a Christian sacrament in the Vulgate and a neutral stone in Wolfram. This site
      reports what each text claims about transcendence and does not rule on whether transcendence
      is real, or import one text&rsquo;s theology into another&rsquo;s. Comparing metaphysics is
      the interesting work. Harmonising them is not.
    </p>

    <h2>What is evoked and what is never named</h2>
    <p>
      The material here is the pre-1500 canon and its public-domain translations. Archetypes may be
      evoked &mdash; a boy drawing a sword from a stone, a wounded king in a barren land &mdash; but
      no modern adaptation is named or leaned on anywhere on this site: no films, no series, no
      games, no studio designs. Every image here is hand-drawn in code. The reason is partly legal
      and mostly editorial: those associations are extremely strong, and they would quietly become
      the reader&rsquo;s default Arthur, which is the flattening this whole portal exists to resist.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Ornament />

  <div class="grid">
    <a class="card" href={withBase("/method/sources/")}>
      <span class="rubric">Sources</span>
      <h3>Texts, translators and rights</h3>
      <p class="small">Every edition this portal draws on, who translated it, when, and whether it may be republished here in full.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href={withBase("/method/whats-not-here/")}>
      <span class="rubric">Gaps</span>
      <h3>What&rsquo;s not here</h3>
      <p class="small">The honest list. Chrétien&rsquo;s Grail poem, the Vulgate&rsquo;s own quest, and the Middle English poems too rough to quote.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href={withBase("/quotes/")}>
      <span class="rubric">Ledger</span>
      <h3>The quote ledger</h3>
      <p class="small">All {q.length} validated quotations, with the result of the last check against each one.</p>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
